The load-bearing finding: the dual path leaks only in rewriters, not in dispatch
Live dispatch is actually airtight. push_tool_batch_result routes to Role::Tool only when provider_call_id is present; llama.cpp/Ollama always return empty tool_calls → None → system fold. So local hops cannot see a tool frame.


tool_dispatch.rs
Ln 55–59
match provider_call_id.map(str::trim).filter(|s| !s.is_empty()) {
    Some(id) => chat_stack.push(crate::engine::Message::tool(content, id)),
    None => chat_stack.push(crate::engine::Message::system(content)),
}
And the OpenAI pairing invariant holds within a batch: every tool_call — success, failure, duplicate-suppress, web-cap — pushes exactly one result frame before continue. I checked each early-exit path. So the leak surface is precisely the code that rewrites the stack after the fact. That's your answer to §9.3: keep Role::Tool; the dual path will keep leaking only as long as rewriters stay pairing-blind. The fix is one shared "is this row part of a native tool round" predicate, applied in four places — not abandoning the format.

